{
  "requirement": "`AgentSettings<D>` identifies the configurations a domain can use in its cache.\nTBV implements this sealed trait for `AgentConfig` and `AgentWithTools<D>`.\n\n```rust,ignore\npub trait AgentSettings<D: Domain>: Settings<D> { }\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The tests adequately check the three obligations in `requirements.sdk.graph.agent-harness.AgentSettings`. I did not run the doctest. I judged it by reading its source.\n\n**Obligations**\n1. `AgentSettings<D>` identifies the configurations a domain can use in its cache. A configuration that implements it is accepted by `ValidationCache::with_agent(config: impl AgentSettings<D>)`.\n2. The trait is implemented for `AgentConfig` and for `AgentWithTools<D>`.\n3. The trait is sealed, so third parties cannot implement it.\n\n**Coverage**\n- **`AgentConfig` accepted by the cache:** `agent/configured.rs#mod[tests]/fn[bedrock_configuration_attaches_to_a_cache_without_being_persisted]` passes an `AgentConfig::Bedrock` to `ValidationCache::with_agent`. It asserts that the cache stores the config as the agent backend, that the backend is shared between clones, and that the config is not persisted. It then runs `refresh_evidence` through the cache.\n- **`AgentWithTools<D>` accepted by the cache:** `agent/configured.rs#mod[tests]/fn[registered_tools_use_the_current_context_and_ordinary_node_ids]` calls `with_agent(backend.clone().with_tool(...).with_tool(...))`. That makes the settings an `AgentWithTools<D>`. The test asserts that the registered tools reach the requests and that a different tool set gives a different tool list. So the settings are accepted and used.\n- **Sealing:** the `compile_fail` doctest on `agent/config.rs#trait[AgentSettings]` implements `AgentSettings<D>` for a third-party `ThirdPartyConfig`. Its imports are public (`trust_but_verify::agent::AgentSettings` and `trust_but_verify::domain::Domain`), and the supertrait lives in the `pub(crate)` module `sealed`. The only plausible reason for the doctest to fail to compile is therefore the seal. A `compile_fail` doctest is a loose check, since any compile error would satisfy it. I don't treat that as a gap here.\n- **Impls exist:** the impls for `AgentConfig` and `AgentWithTools<D>` are `impl[AgentConfig/AgentSettings<D>]` and `impl[AgentWithTools<D>/AgentSettings<D>]` in `agent/config.rs`. The sealed `Settings<D>` impls for both types are in `agent/domain_tools.rs#mod[sealed]`. Both tests compile and use these impls through `with_agent`.\n\nThe requirement has no other behavior, error case or boundary. The tests carry `{test}` references to it, and no `{fixme}` acknowledgments apply.",
  "references": "cec8dc27c3b2606c94129fd6e5a30835b88f6c749b7486e187768c03410c8bbd",
